#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a60886ab-a9e4-5c51-8ce4-015666d95c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307350} {:answer/id #uuid "dd857f6a-0056-526a-8bd7-e08cb1377977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/8f43f19bbe4213ba2f9d5bc51f52b747e1c7840a27607207f0ca56021c1914d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307351} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd696d9-06d4-5a69-bb5e-d38cd9f6d604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/0f0589a467b3d35947a6f501ff00d5296e05ae7e375800659986038db55c7577.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307352} {:answer/id #uuid "7a38e9d6-28b6-54a1-8f99-6a0757341358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/378a24a8553ac7092b85f715394bf632b5e87aaf45c58ce3ff7ae66310b28f65.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307353} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8d6760-24d7-594e-af6e-1901cfb5b927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/95fbb1a2298ebc809303d6c57a8440483cdd65748b090ea0b45d25ebad93bb80.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a60886ab-a9e4-5c51-8ce4-015666d95c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307350} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3aee704-3c44-51a1-b1a0-55744186f527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "babac56b-1bc2-59da-833f-f3070de287aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-147603" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "713fc33d-83da-590f-9f68-03deb34d7745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308835} {:answer/id #uuid "896cd95c-a469-50db-a939-47330ea46144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308836} {:answer/id #uuid "44143780-c8cd-55cd-9fe2-f289fdbbbfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308837} {:answer/id #uuid "c69d2fdb-8d95-5e19-9150-433c74826146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308838} {:answer/id #uuid "5a10463e-18ff-55ca-a408-e678a6c87bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a10463e-18ff-55ca-a408-e678a6c87bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308839} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8deeb3c-b80e-5f4d-baab-4e4a74ff10dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49164e78-91b8-549a-8993-e1fab20375b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87125" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/c010f583fc33ee3c5335c16c0aaf1c20c2e5cf82d49fe7cf14e8e5661bf202fc.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$"}]}
